{%- set churchIntro -%}<p class="page-intro">St James the Great is the parish church of Harvington, a short drive south-east of the Lenches, and the H in the ARCH Benefice. Its green copper spire makes it easy to spot. The benefice's midweek Eucharist is held here, and the church is listed Grade I.</p>{%- endset -%}
{%- set churchAbout -%}<h2>A short history</h2>
    <p>The lower part of the tower, and its doorway into the nave, are 12th century and the oldest parts of the building; marks on the west wall may point to an even earlier Saxon church. The nave was widened and given new windows in the 14th century.</p>
    <p>In 1855 Frederick Preedy restored the church for about £1,750, adding the spire and the clock. The spire was first covered in oak shingles, and has been clad in copper since 1947, which has weathered to its distinctive green.</p>
    <h2>What to look out for</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The simple tub-shaped font, made in the 12th century.</li>
      <li>Preedy's east window, restored in 2008, which is on the Evesham Preedy trail.</li>
      <li>The six bells, recast by John Taylor &amp; Co in 1855 and rehung for full-circle ringing in 1998.</li>
      <li>The memorial to Thomas Feriman (died 1619) on the chancel wall, framed by columns.</li>
    </ul>{%- endset -%}
